.Dd October 9, 2026
.Dt LIBTIMECMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm timecmp_client_open ,
.Nm timecmp_client_create ,
.Nm timecmp_client_close ,
.Nm timecmp_get ,
.Nm timecmp_set ,
.Nm timecmp_adjust
.Nd client for the system.Time capability
.Sh LIBRARY
System time client library
.Pq Fl l Ns Li timecmp
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In timecmp.h
.Ft int
.Fn timecmp_client_open "struct timecmp_client **clientp"
.Ft int
.Fn timecmp_client_create "int fd" "struct timecmp_client **clientp"
.Ft void
.Fn timecmp_client_close "struct timecmp_client *client"
.Ft int
.Fn timecmp_get "struct timecmp_client *client" "struct timespec *ts"
.Ft int
.Fn timecmp_set "struct timecmp_client *client" "const struct timespec *ts"
.Ft int
.Fn timecmp_adjust "struct timecmp_client *client" "const struct timeval *delta" "struct timeval *old"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm libtimecmp
is the client to
.Xr BSDTime 8 ,
the
.Dq system.Time
capability provider.
A holder of a system.Time channel reads the wall clock with
.Fn timecmp_get
and, when the provider's per-label policy grants it set authority, steps the
clock with
.Fn timecmp_set
.Po Xr clock_settime 2 , Dv CLOCK_REALTIME Pc
or slews it with
.Fn timecmp_adjust
.Pq Xr adjtime 2 .
Setting and slewing need
.Dv PRIV_SETTIMEOFDAY ,
which a sandboxed caller does not hold; the broker performs the privileged
operation after a per-label policy check.
Reading the clock is unprivileged and allowed to any holder.
.Pp
.Fn timecmp_client_open
opens a session and returns 0, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Fn timecmp_set
and
.Fn timecmp_adjust
fail with
.Er EPERM
when the caller's label is not granted set authority.
.Pp
.Fn timecmp_client_create
adopts an already held native channel, consuming
.Fa fd
only on success.
The connection is non-transferable and locked against exec and fork inheritance.
The object is owned by its creating process and a single dispatch thread;
calls from an inherited object fail with
.Er ECHILD .
.Pp
The implementation uses the generated C bindings for
.Li bsd.time.v1 .
The first operation negotiates wire version 1, within the same five-second
monotonic deadline as the operation itself.
A timeout closes the connection and is reported as
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
Clock writes are never retried automatically; a transport failure does not prove
that a sent write was not applied.
Malformed responses close the connection with
.Er EPROTO .
An incompatible advertised version reports
.Er EPROTONOSUPPORT .
The signed seconds and microseconds of a slew are preserved, including negative
fractions in the range -999999 through 999999.
.Ss Migration status
The source implementation has migrated to FIDL; production activation and
capability-plane VM validation remain incomplete.
The public C ABI remains version 1.
The old wire validator is retained for ABI compatibility, but the provider
rejects the legacy wire format.
Provider and client-library packages must be deployed or rolled back together,
with their processes restarted; mixed wire versions fail before a clock write.
Rollback must follow the bundle registry's increasing-sequence policy.
.Sh PACKAGES
The runtime library is in
.Li 5BSD-libtimecmp .
The
.Li 5BSD-libtimecmp-dev
package supplies public headers, the static library, the FIDL schema, and
required development-package dependencies.
Manuals and split debugging symbols are in
.Li 5BSD-libtimecmp-man
and
.Li 5BSD-libtimecmp-dbg .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr adjtime 2 ,
.Xr clock_settime 2 ,
.Xr BSDTime 8
